# Paths semgrep does not scan.
#
# Everything here is a PATH exclusion, never a rule suppression. A rule that
# fires on code we keep is silenced at the line itself with
# `# nosemgrep: <rule id> -- <why>`, so the reason travels with the code and
# dies with it. Nothing in this file disables a rule anywhere else.

# Dependencies and build output. Not our source, and their vulnerabilities are
# pip-audit's, npm audit's and trivy's job rather than semgrep's.
node_modules/
.venv/
venv/
dist/
build/
*.min.js
*.map

# Caches, reports and scanner output.
.git/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/
__pycache__/
htmlcov/
coverage/
reports/

# Generated ADR renderings: self-contained HTML with the Mermaid sources
# inlined, which semgrep's HTML parser chokes on. The hand-written source is
# docs/adr/*.md, which IS scanned.
docs/adr/html/

# kind's generated kubeconfigs for the local development fleet. Gitignored,
# never shipped, and rewritten by every `make fleet-up`.
fleet/kubeconfigs/
